Q: I have two different types of condition in my model to check lr_no number how can I check in if else condition in controller A: <p>Your model methods are violating the Single Responsibility Principle, the methods names do a poor job of expressing what the methods do, and the methods appear to be performing business logic (not the responsibility of the model).</p>
<p>Your two methods should split into four methods and each should return a raw payload (devoid of business logic).</p>
<p>It is highly unusual to join many tables simply to return the record count, so I am going to default to returning tabular data.</p>
<p>Call sites, like controller methods, should be able to call the model functions independently and as needed.</p>
<p>As alien as your business logic might be to outside developers (like me), your model methods should be immediately clear and indicative about what they do.</p>
<p>Model</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public function getAllCrossReceived(): array
{
    return $this-&gt;db
        -&gt;join('crossing_received cr', 'cr.crossing_rec_frtno = tf.fr_memo_ids')
        -&gt;get_where(
            'truck_freight tf',
            ['crossing_rec_type' =&gt; 'cross_received']
        )
        -&gt;result();
}

